 ('REF-00945','journal_article','Rantakokko','Merja','Rantakokko M, Iwarsson S, Kauppinen M, Leinonen R, Heikkinen E, Rantanen T',2010,
  'Quality of life and barriers in the urban outdoor environment in old age','Journal of the American Geriatrics Society','58','11','2154','2159',NULL,
  NULL,'10.1111/j.1532-5415.2010.03143.x','21054297',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','FI',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI and PMID both confirmed against the record.',
  'Cross-sectional, n=589 community-dwelling people aged 75-81, Finland. LISREL path model. Environmental barriers measured as self-reported lack of resting places or long distances (distances), noisy traffic/dangerous crossroads (traffic), hilly terrain/poor street condition (terrain). Terrain and traffic influenced QoL THROUGH fear of moving outdoors and unmet physical activity need; distances had a DIRECT association with QoL. Model explained 36% of QoL variance.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating'),

 ('REF-00946','journal_article','Rantakokko','Merja','Rantakokko M, Iwarsson S, Hirvensalo M, Leinonen R, Heikkinen E, Rantanen T',2010,
  'Unmet physical activity need in old age','Journal of the American Geriatrics Society','58','4','707','712',NULL,
  NULL,'10.1111/j.1532-5415.2010.02792.x','20398151',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','FI',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI and PMID both confirmed against the record.',
  'Prospective cohort + cross-sectional, n=643 baseline / n=314 at 2-year follow-up, aged 75-81, Finland. All participants could walk at least 500m outdoors at baseline; 14% still perceived unmet physical activity need. LACK OF RESTING PLACES correlated with unmet physical activity need at baseline, association ESPECIALLY STRONG in those with walking difficulties. Direct evidence that the rest-point demand is not confined to people who cannot walk far.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating'),

 ('REF-00947','journal_article','Rosenberg','Dori','Rosenberg DE, Huang DL, Simonovich SD, Belza B',2012,
  'Outdoor built environment barriers and facilitators to activity among midlife and older adults with mobility disabilities','The Gerontologist','53','2','268','279',NULL,
  NULL,'10.1093/geront/gns119','23010096',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','US',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI and PMID both confirmed against the record.',
  'Qualitative, n=35 adults over 50 (mean 67, range 50-86) using assistive devices (canes 57%, walkers 57%, wheelchairs 46%), King County WA, US. GPS-prompted in-depth interviews, latent content analysis, dual independent coding. AVAILABILITY OF RESTING PLACES AND SHELTER ON STREETS named as a key theme alongside curb ramps, sidewalk condition, hills, lighting. CLASSIFIED T3 NOT Co-1: investigator-led academic qualitative research with disabled participants is not a disability-led publication; see tier-system.md Co-1 definition.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating'),

 ('REF-00948','journal_article','Portegijs','Erja','Portegijs E, Rantakokko M, Edgren J, Salpakoski A, Heinonen A, Arkela M, Kallinen M, Rantanen T, Sipila S',2013,
  'Effects of a rehabilitation program on perceived environmental barriers in older patients recovering from hip fracture: a randomized controlled trial','BioMed Research International','2013',NULL,NULL,NULL,'769645',
  NULL,'10.1155/2013/769645','23986910','PMC3748419',NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','FI',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI, PMID and PMCID confirmed against the record.',
  'RCT secondary analysis (ISRCTN53680197), n=81 community-dwelling hip-fracture patients, mean 70 days post-trauma, Finland. TIERED T3 NOT T1 DELIBERATELY: the tier definition requires intervention-level control ON THE PARAMETER UNDER DESIGN. The trial randomised a 12-month individual home-based rehabilitation programme, not the built environment; relative to rest-point provision it is uncontrolled. NEGATIVE RESULT / R7 INADEQUACY FINDING: intervention had no additional benefit over standard care on perceived outdoor barriers (time p=0.199, group p=0.911, interaction p=0.430); 60-62% still perceived at least one outdoor barrier incl. lack of resting places. Evidence that the intervention point is environmental provision, not individual capacity.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating'),

 ('REF-00949','standard','Department for Transport',NULL,'Department for Transport (UK)',2021,
  'Inclusive Mobility: A Guide to Best Practice on Access to Pedestrian and Transport Infrastructure',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,
  'Department for Transport',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/61d32bb7d3bf7f1f72b5ffd2/inclusive-mobility-a-guide-to-best-practice-on-access-to-pedestrian-and-transport-infrastructure.pdf','2026-07-25',5,'national_fw','UK',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED',NULL,'WebFetch+zlib-text-extraction','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'PDF retrieved 2026-07-25 and text extracted directly; all clause citations below read from the document body, not from secondary description. Crown copyright 2021, OGL v3.0.',
  'CLAUSE-CITED VALUES (R3 satisfied). Section 4.5 Seating, page 31, verbatim: "Mobility impaired people need seating at reasonably frequent intervals. In commonly used pedestrian areas, and transport interchanges and stations, seats should be provided at intervals of no more than 50 metres. Wherever possible, seats should also be provided at bus stops and shelters. Seating should be placed adjacent to, but not obstructing, the pedestrian route and should be picked out in contrasting colours to help people with visual impairment." || Page 102 (waiting/refreshment areas): majority of seats seat height 470-480mm; armrests about 200mm above seat level; within any row either ALL or NO seats have armrests because mixed designs cause difficulties for vision impaired people; minimum seat width 500mm; perch seating 700mm; fold-down seats 550-600mm; outdoor seats must shed rainwater; vandal-resistant easy-clean; tonal and colour contrast. || Page 124 (countryside/rural paths): "Seats and perches should be placed at regular intervals along paths in the countryside. They should be located no more than 100 metres apart and set back from the main route by at least 600mm"; surfaced resting places at least 900mm square beside seats so wheelchair users can sit next to family and friends. || NOTE THE INTERNAL DIVERGENCE: 50m urban (s4.5) vs 100m countryside (p124), same document.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating'),

 ('REF-00950','guideline','Wheels for Wellbeing',NULL,'Wheels for Wellbeing',2025,
  'Benches and Seating in Public Spaces',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,
  'Wheels for Wellbeing',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://wheelsforwellbeing.org.uk/our-campaigns/resources/benches-and-seating-in-public-spaces/','2026-07-25',NULL,'co1','UK',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED',NULL,'WebFetch','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Co-1 STATUS VERIFIED BY SEPARATE RE-RETRIEVAL, NOT ASSUMED. The organisation About page (re-retrieved 2026-07-25) states verbatim: "Informed by life-changing personal experience of Disabled trustees, staff and volunteers" and "All our work is informed by practical experience of Disabled people discovering, using and enjoying cycling". The page does NOT use the phrase "disabled-led", so that phrase is not asserted on the organisation behalf. Registered charity England and Wales no. 1120905. Guidance updated 11/2025.',
  'Co-1 named-disability-organisation output. SPECIFICATIONS: seat height 470-480mm typical BUT ALSO a deliberate plurality of 380mm / 480mm / 580mm so that one height does not exclude; perch 700mm; seat depth 380-470mm (HSE), flip-down 300-400mm, 420-450mm recommended max; seat width at least 500mm, 600-700mm min for broader accessibility; seat angle +5 to -5 degrees; seat-to-back gap under 100mm; armrest 200mm above seat, diameter 32-50mm, length at least 80% to front of seat, 500-700mm between paired armrests; 1500mm min bench length from a single armrest for leg extension; 500-750mm clear enables wheelchair transfer without an end armrest; backrest 300mm min above seat, 500-600mm max, angle 90-100 degrees from seat; SPACE UNDER BENCH matters for assistance dogs and for people who sit and manoeuvre with feet under a bench; setback at least 1200mm from route, absolute min 600mm; transfer space at least 1.2m either side (2m one side if cycle parking); base gradient 1:100-1:60, never steeper than 1:60; picnic table knee space 750mm high x 500mm deep x 900mm wide; bins not closer than about 5m; benches spaced no more than 50m apart. || INDEPENDENCE WARNING (adversarial pass, DR-2026-05-09): the 50m spacing figure is NOT independent Co-1 evidence. WfW own reference list cites DfT Inclusive Mobility (2021) alongside BS8300-1:2018, HSE HSG57, ADK, ADM, LTN 1/20, BS4800, Blackler et al 2018, DfT Reference Wheelchair Report 2022, City of York Accessible Seating Review. Treating WfW 50m as corroboration of DfT 50m would double-count one figure.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating'),

 ('REF-00951','grey','American Society of Landscape Architects',NULL,'American Society of Landscape Architects',NULL,
  'Universal Design Guide - Streets',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,
  'ASLA',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.asla.org/focus-areas/diversity,-equity,-inclusion/universal-design-guide/universal-design-guide-streets','2026-07-25',3,'grey','US',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED',NULL,'WebFetch','2026-07-25','medium',1,
  'Retrieved 2026-07-25. No publication date stated on the page. Admitted as grey (T3-grey, weak band) specifically so the mis-citation it propagates is documented in the corpus rather than left to be rediscovered.',
  '[UNVERIFIED-QUANT]. Asserts verbatim under "Frequent seating with arms": "Providing places to rest, benches or low retaining walls, at least every 65 feet (20 meters) along street edges increases the number of older adults and mobility-disabled people who feel comfortable traveling by sidewalk." Its SOLE citation is a hyperlink to PMC5549237 (= REF-00952), with no bibliographic detail given. REF-00952 was re-retrieved in full: it contains NO bench-spacing figure of any kind, and no disabled cohort. BOTH the 20m quantity AND the "mobility-disabled people" population attribution are unsupported by the cited source. Do not propagate this figure.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating'),

 ('REF-00952','journal_article',NULL,NULL,NULL,2016,
  'Improving walking conditions for older adults. A three-step method investigation','European Journal of Ageing',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,
  NULL,'10.1007/s10433-015-0340-5','28804358','PMC5549237','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5549237/','2026-07-25',3,'clinical','NO',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_full_text_article+WebFetch','2026-07-25','medium',0,
  'Retrieved via PubMed full-text tool and the PMC article page 2026-07-25. AUTHOR NAMES NOT CAPTURED by either retrieval path and are deliberately left NULL rather than guessed; author backfill is an open metadata task on this row.',
  'Mixed-method (survey n=1,761; participatory observation n=44; workshop), Kristiansand, Norway. Participants aged 67+ living in their own homes, mean age 76.1; paper states "the least mobile groups living in institutions are not represented". CONTAINS NO QUANTIFIED BENCH-SPACING FIGURE. Only bench passage is qualitative: "Benches are a measure that can be helpful for elders with reduced motor skills and balance. During the participatory observation, many suggested places where they would want a bench situated, usually close to steep gradients and especially in areas outside the city centre." Older elders rated benches significantly more important than younger groups. This row exists to anchor the REF-00951 mis-citation finding.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating');

